= i
{parent=2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A positive density factorizes according to an <undirected graphical model> $G^u=(\mathcal V,\mathcal E)$ when there are nonnegative <clique potentials> $\psi_C$ such that
$$
f(v)=\frac1Z\prod_{C\in\mathcal C}\psi_C(v_C),
$$
where $\mathcal C$ may be taken as the maximal cliques and $Z$ normalizes the density. Equivalently, each potential involves only variables in one complete subgraph.

= b
{parent=i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <global Markov property for an undirected graph> says that whenever a vertex set $S$ separates disjoint sets $A$ and $B$ in the graph,
$$
V_A\mathrel{\perp\!\!\!\perp}V_B\mid V_S.
$$
The <Hammersley-Clifford theorem> states that a strictly positive density factorizes over the cliques of an undirected graph if and only if it satisfies this global Markov property. Strict positivity is essential for the converse from conditional independences to factorization.

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $K=\Sigma^{-1}$ be the <precision matrix>, let $R=\{1,\ldots,p\}\setminus\{j,k\}$, and condition on $V_R=v_R$. In the Gaussian exponent, all terms depending jointly on $v_j$ and $v_k$ are contained in
$$
-\frac12\left(K_{jj}v_j^2+2K_{jk}v_jv_k+K_{kk}v_k^2
+2b_j(v_R)v_j+2b_k(v_R)v_k\right).
$$
If $K_{jk}=0$, this conditional density is a product of one function of $v_j$ and one function of $v_k$, so the <conditional independence> of $V_j$ and $V_k$ given $V_R$ holds.

Conversely, conditional independence makes this everywhere-positive conditional density factorize. Its mixed second derivative must therefore vanish:
$$
\frac{\partial^2}{\partial v_j\partial v_k}
\log f(v_j,v_k\mid v_R)=-K_{jk}=0.
$$
Hence the conditional independence holds exactly when $(\Sigma^{-1})_{jk}=0$.

= iii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

A density factorizes according to a <directed acyclic graph> when
$$
f(v_1,\ldots,v_p)=\prod_{j=1}^p
f(v_j\mid v_{\operatorname{pa}(j)}),
$$
where $\operatorname{pa}(j)$ is the set of parents of vertex $j$.

The required undirected graph is the <moral graph>: join every pair of parents having a common child, retain the parent-child adjacencies, and remove all arrowheads. Each DAG family $\{j\}\cup\operatorname{pa}(j)$ is then a clique, so each conditional factor is a clique potential and the DAG factorization is also an undirected factorization. These edges are minimal for a guarantee covering every DAG-factorizing density, because an arbitrary conditional factor can couple every pair of variables in its family.

= 3
{parent=Paper 221}
{scope}

= i
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A <nonparametric structural equation model> for the graph can be written
$$
X=f_X(U_X),\qquad A=f_A(U_A),\qquad
M=f_M(A,X,U_M),\qquad Y=f_Y(M,X,U_Y).
$$
The bidirected edge $A\leftrightarrow Y$ permits dependence between $U_A$ and $U_Y$; apart from this pair the exogenous variables are mutually independent. The basic <potential outcomes> are
$$
M(a,x)=f_M(a,x,U_M),
\qquad
Y(m,x)=f_Y(m,x,U_Y),
$$
and the natural nested outcome is $Y(a)=Y(M(a,X),X)$.

The exogenous independences imply that the whole family $\{M(a,x):a,x\}$ is independent of $\{X,A,Y(m,x):m,x\}$, while $X$ is independent of $A$ and the basic response potentials. The bidirected edge means that $A$ and $Y(m,x)$ need not be independent. Useful observed-counterfactual consequences include
$$
M(a)\mathrel{\perp\!\!\!\perp}A\mid X,
\qquad
M\mathrel{\perp\!\!\!\perp}Y(m)\mid A,X.
$$

= ii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

In an <acyclic directed mixed graph>, a <fixable vertex> $v$ is one whose bidirected district contains no proper directed descendant of $v$:
$$
\operatorname{dis}(v)\cap\operatorname{de}(v)=\{v\}.
$$
Here $A$ and $Y$ lie in the same district because $A\leftrightarrow Y$, and $Y$ is a directed descendant of $A$ along $A\to M\to Y$. Thus $A$ is not fixable.

= iii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= a
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The claim is not implied. On the path $A\leftrightarrow Y\leftarrow X$, $Y$ is a <collider>, and conditioning on $Y$ opens that path. Equivalently, conditioning on the common effect $Y$ can induce <collider bias> between $A$ and $X$.

= b
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The claim is not implied. In the graph for $Y(m)$ the path
$$
M\leftarrow A\leftrightarrow Y(m)
$$
is open after conditioning only on $X$. The latent common cause represented by the bidirected edge associates $A$ with $Y(m)$, and $A$ also causes $M$.

= c
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The claim is not implied. The intervention setting $A=a$ removes the outgoing dependence of descendants on the observed value of $A$, but the bidirected path $A\leftrightarrow Y(a)$ remains open. Conditioning on $X$ does not block latent confounding between $A$ and $Y$.

= d
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

This conditional independence is implied. Fixing the mediator at $m$ removes the directed edge $M\to Y$ in the relevant counterfactual graph. Conditioning on $A$ blocks $M\leftarrow A\leftrightarrow Y(m)$, while conditioning on $X$ blocks $M\leftarrow X\to Y(m)$. Every remaining path is blocked by the <m-separation> criterion, so
$$
M\mathrel{\perp\!\!\!\perp}Y(m)\mid A,X.
$$

= e
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

This conditional independence is implied. In the graph for $M(a)$, the incoming causal value of $A$ has been fixed, so the path $A\to M$ no longer transmits association to $M(a)$. The only possible route through $Y$ contains a collider, and conditioning on $X$ blocks the common-cause path through $X$. Hence <m-separation> gives
$$
A\mathrel{\perp\!\!\!\perp}M(a)\mid X.
$$

= iv
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

This is the covariate-conditional <front-door adjustment>. The paths from $A$ to $M$ have no unblocked backdoor path after conditioning on $X$, and all backdoor paths from $M$ to $Y$ are blocked by $(A,X)$. Therefore
$$
\mathbb P(Y(a)=y)
=\sum_{x,m}\mathbb P(x)\mathbb P(m\mid a,x)
\sum_{a'}\mathbb P(y\mid m,a',x)\mathbb P(a'\mid x).
$$
The inner sum identifies the effect of setting $M=m$ at covariate value $x$ by adjusting for $A$; the outer sum transports this through the mediator distribution generated by setting $A=a$ and then averages over $X$.

= 4
{parent=Paper 221}
{scope}

= i
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For binary $A$, conditional covariance satisfies
$$
\operatorname{Cov}(A,Y\mid X)
=\pi(X)\{1-\pi(X)\}
\left\{\mathbb E(Y\mid A=1,X)-\mathbb E(Y\mid A=0,X)\right\}.
$$
By <conditional exchangeability> and <consistency of potential outcomes>, the difference in braces is $\mathbb E[Y(1)-Y(0)\mid X]$. Consequently
$$
\beta
=\mathbb E\!\left[
\pi(X)\{1-\pi(X)\}\{Y(1)-Y(0)\}
\right],
$$
so the required weight is
$$
w(X)=\pi(X)\{1-\pi(X)\}.
$$
This <overlap weight> emphasizes covariate strata with treatment probabilities near one half and downweights strata near a violation of <positivity in causal inference>.

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Write $p_x=\mathbb P(X=x)$, $\pi_x=\mathbb E(A\mid X=x)$, and $\mu_x=\mathbb E(Y\mid X=x)$. The functional is
$$
\beta=\mathbb E(AY)-\sum_xp_x\pi_x\mu_x.
$$
The first term has <influence function> $AY-\mathbb E(AY)$. Applying the product rule to the second term, including perturbations of $p_x$, $\pi_x$, and $\mu_x$, gives
$$
\pi(X)\mu(X)-\mathbb E[\pi(X)\mu(X)]
+\mu(X)\{A-\pi(X)\}
+\pi(X)\{Y-\mu(X)\}.
$$
Subtracting and using $\beta=\mathbb E(AY)-\mathbb E[\pi\mu]$ yields
$$
\psi(X,A,Y)
=\{A-\pi(X)\}\{Y-\mu(X)\}-\beta.
$$
Its expectation is zero because the first product has expectation $\mathbb E\{\operatorname{Cov}(A,Y\mid X)\}=\beta$.

= iii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Condition on the independent external sample, and abbreviate $\delta_\pi=\widehat\pi_m-\pi$ and $\delta_\mu=\widehat\mu_m-\mu$. Expanding the <plug-in estimator> around the true nuisance functions gives
$$
\widehat\beta-\beta
=(\mathbb P_n-P)\{(A-\pi)(Y-\mu)\}
+P(\delta_\pi\delta_\mu)+R_{n,m},
$$
where $\sqrt nR_{n,m}=o_p(1)$ because the two nuisance mean squared errors vanish, the evaluation sample is independent of the nuisance fits, $A$ is bounded, and $Y$ has bounded support. The linear term is the empirical average of the <influence function>, so the <central limit theorem> gives
$$
\sqrt n(\mathbb P_n-P)\{(A-\pi)(Y-\mu)\}
\xrightarrow dN(0,\operatorname{Var}\psi).
$$
The remaining bias obeys the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality>
$$
|P(\delta_\pi\delta_\mu)|
\leq\sqrt{\operatorname{MSE}(\widehat\pi_m)
\operatorname{MSE}(\widehat\mu_m)}.
$$
Thus the claimed conclusion follows under the standard product-rate condition
$$
n\operatorname{MSE}(\widehat\pi_m)
\operatorname{MSE}(\widehat\mu_m)\longrightarrow0,
$$
equivalently $\sqrt n\sqrt{\operatorname{MSE}(\widehat\pi_m)operatorname{MSE}(\widehat\mu_m)}\to0$. <Slutsky theorem> then yields
$$
\sqrt n(\widehat\beta-\beta)
\xrightarrow dN(0,\operatorname{Var}(\psi(X,A,Y))).
$$

As printed, the paper instead assumes only $\sqrt n\operatorname{MSE}(\widehat\pi_m)\operatorname{MSE}(\widehat\mu_m)\to0$, which is insufficient with its stated definition of MSE. For example, deterministic nuisance errors of size $n^{-3/16}$ have both MSEs equal to $n^{-3/8}$ and satisfy the printed condition, while the scaled product bias is $\sqrt n\,n^{-3/8}=n^{1/8}$. The result therefore requires the stronger condition above, or “MSE” in the printed rate must be read as root mean squared error.
